    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0d3f0646-7d9a-501d-85e0-da8c48c7a232",
      ":question/problem": "Let $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $b$ and points the same direction.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|b| &= \\sqrt{(-12)^2+(-5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{144+25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{169} \\\\\n&= 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{b}{|b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13}\\cdot (b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{12}{13} \\\\ - \\frac{5}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/requires-calculator": false,
