      ":question/problem": "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+b &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0+(8) \\\\ 6+(-12)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2a+b| &= \\sqrt{(8)^2+(-6)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u &= \\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (2a+b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
